## 9. Hardware readings are not safety controls

Flux displays readings reported by hardware, firmware, drivers, and the operating system. Sensor availability and accuracy depend on hardware, firmware, drivers, and permissions. Readings can be unavailable, delayed, or incorrect. Do not use Flux as the sole control for safety-critical systems or protection against hardware damage. Alerts and thresholds are conveniences and can fail to appear.

Do not rely on Flux to prevent overheating, data loss, equipment damage, or personal injury, or for any medical, life-support, industrial-control, or other use where a failure could cause harm.

## 10. System changes, drivers and administrator actions

Some Flux features change your system. Depending on edition and the options you choose, Flux or its installer can add a startup entry, add or remove a Windows Firewall rule for remote sharing, install a Windows sensor service, start an elevated sensor helper, and run the official PawnIO installer. Actions that need administrator rights proceed only after Windows administrator approval. Flux identifies those actions in its interface.

On Windows, CPU temperature readings require the third-party PawnIO kernel driver. The standalone installer offers PawnIO as an unticked option, and either edition can set it up later, only after you approve it in Flux. You can decline administrator approval; CPU temperature readings are then unavailable. A kernel driver runs with high privileges. You decide whether to approve it on each computer, and you are responsible for approving it only where you are allowed to. The shared PawnIO driver can remain installed after Flux closes or is removed, and other software may use it. Manage or remove it separately in Windows.

## 11. Storage cleanup and deletion

Pro storage tools can permanently delete data when you instruct them to. Depending on the version, they can delete selected temporary files from your user temporary folder, run a temporary-file cleanup on a schedule you turn on, empty the Recycle Bin of a drive you select, and run Windows component-cleanup commands. One of those commands removes the ability to go back to a previous Windows installation. Items removed by these tools may not be recoverable by Flux, by the Recycle Bin, or otherwise.

These tools act only on your instruction, including a schedule you enable. Review what you select before you confirm. You are responsible for the choices you make and for keeping backups of anything important. Flux does not promise that a cleanup will free a particular amount of space or finish every item.

## 12. Remote sharing and pairing

Remote sharing is off until you turn it on. When it is on, Flux shares this computer's sensor readings, including drive and network-adapter names, over your network with viewers you pair or give a key to. You are responsible for deciding who may view your computer and for the network you share on.

Only enable remote broadcasting on systems and networks you are authorized to monitor. Protect pairing credentials and obtain any permissions required to share readings. Keep pairing codes and keys confidential. Remove viewers you no longer trust, and regenerate keys if a key may have been exposed. Pairing codes, keys, and viewer lists stay on the computers involved; the publisher cannot see, reset, or recover them. Firewalls, VPNs, routing, and port forwarding outside Flux can change who can reach a shared computer.

## 13. Downloads, installer warnings and updates

The standalone installer is not code-signed. Windows SmartScreen, antivirus, or other security software may warn about it, block it, or quarantine it. The publisher cannot promise that any security product will accept Flux. Download Flux only from the official channels in section 2 and compare the published SHA-256 checksum before running the installer. A checksum published with the same release confirms that the file is intact, not who published it.

The standalone edition can check GitHub for new versions as described in the Privacy Policy. It installs an update only after you choose to. Microsoft Store manages updates for Store editions. Updates may add, change, or remove features. The publisher is not required to provide updates, and may stop providing a version, edition, or distribution channel, subject to applicable law and any rights you already paid for.

